title: $:/plugins/nikorion/font-manager/language/en-GB/readme
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A web font manager for TiddlyWiki. Drop a font file (`.woff2`, `.woff`, `.ttf`, `.otf`) onto the wiki: it becomes a self-contained font tiddler holding a `@font-face` rule with the font embedded in base64 — no network request, works offline and in single-file wikis.

!! Font tiddler

```css
title: $:/fonts/<Name>
tags:  $:/tags/Stylesheet
type:  text/css

@font-face {
    font-family: "<Name>";
    font-weight: normal;
    font-style: normal;
    src: url(data:font/woff2;base64,<<<LONG_BASE64_CODE>>>) format("woff2");
}
```

! Using the font

Drag a font file onto the wiki and import it from the usual import screen. Once imported, the font is available to any CSS in the wiki. Reference it by name with `font-family`, for example in a stylesheet tiddler:

```css
.tc-tiddler-title { font-family: "Lobster"; }
```

Or inline in wikitext:

```xml
@@font-family:"Caveat Brush";
Some fancy text.
@@
```

Imported fonts are always stored as `text/css` tiddlers. This is not mandatory for a stylesheet, but with the default wikitext type TiddlyWiki would parse the very long base64 data as wikitext (which would cause slowness, and could even corrupt the font if the base64 contains a `//` sequence, read as italic markup).

The plugin itself ships no fonts, but it offers some conveniences: a filterable list -- by various criteria -- of the fonts imported with the manager (those whose title starts with `$:/fonts/`), and their editing, to set their type by hand (sans, serif, mono, script, fantasy), give them a note or a rank (★ to ★★★★), and record their licence and source.

Online font collections: [[Dafont|https://www.dafont.com/]] • [[FontLibrary|https://fontlibrary.org/]] • [[FontSquirrel|https://www.fontsquirrel.com/]] • [[Google Fonts|https://fonts.google.com/]]

! WOFF2 conversion

`.ttf` and `.otf` files are converted to WOFF2 while they are imported (typically 40 to 80 % smaller). The conversion can be turned off in ''Settings'' > ''Font Manager''. `.woff` (WOFF 1) files are not converted: they are already compressed, so they would gain too little from WOFF2.

The conversion relies on the [[woff2-encoder|https://github.com/itskyedo/woff2-encoder]] library (MIT licence), a WebAssembly WOFF2 encoder bundled in the plugin. It was chosen because it is entirely self-contained: the WebAssembly module is inlined in the file, with no download and no server. The conversion therefore runs in the browser, offline and in a single-file wiki, like the rest of the plugin. As it weighs about 1 MB, it is only loaded when you import a `.ttf` or `.otf` file, and only once per browsing session.

! Saving to disk

With a wiki served by Node.js, the imported font is written next to your other tiddlers, in the `tiddlers/` folder, as a flattened file such as `$__fonts_Lobster.css`. To group the fonts in a sub-folder, write this line in a tiddler titled `$:/config/FileSystemPaths`:

```xml
[prefix[$:/fonts/]search-replace:g:regexp[/|\\],[_]addprefix[system/fonts/]]
```

The font files will then be redirected to `tiddlers/system/fonts/`. If a `$:/config/FileSystemPaths` tiddler already exists, add the line above the others, since the first ones take priority. This is a TiddlyWiki setting: a plugin cannot do it itself; single-file wikis are not concerned.
